What costs.py doesn't cover: a stream kept forever with SHARDS, a SYNC view on a
provisioned table, SPLIT, WHERE and ARRAY JOIN, uniq and quantile sketches, a Float64 avg,
and queries that read Array parameters, literal lists, residual filters and a sharded
stream.
-- dynaflux.yaml --
store: shop
regions: [us-east-1, eu-west-1]
archive:
  events: local
tables:
  customer_totals:
    capacity: provisioned
    autoscaling:
      read:  {min: 5, max: 500}
      write: {min: 5, max: 500, target_percent: 50}
-- dynaflux/orders.sql --
CREATE STREAM orders (
    customer_id String,
    store_id    UInt32,
    amount      Decimal(12, 2),
    price       Float64,
    items       Array(String),
    latency_ms  UInt32
)
PRIMARY KEY (customer_id)
SHARDS 4
LOG RETENTION FOREVER;
-- dynaflux/views.sql --
CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW customer_totals
MODE SYNC
ORDER BY (customer_id)
AS SELECT customer_id, sum(amount) AS spent, avg(price) AS avg_price, count() AS orders
FROM orders
GROUP BY customer_id;

CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW store_hourly
ORDER BY (store_id, hour)
PRIMARY KEY (store_id)
TTL hour + INTERVAL 30 DAY
SPLIT 4
AS SELECT
    store_id,
    toStartOfHour(event_time)  AS hour,
    uniq(customer_id)          AS customers,
    quantile(0.99)(latency_ms) AS p99,
    countIf(price > 100)       AS big_orders
FROM orders
WHERE amount > 0
GROUP BY store_id, hour;

CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW item_daily
ORDER BY (item, day)
PRIMARY KEY (item)
AS SELECT item, toStartOfDay(event_time) AS day, count() AS n
FROM orders
ARRAY JOIN items AS item
GROUP BY item, day;
-- dynaflux/queries.sql --
-- name: StoreHours :many
SELECT hour, customers
FROM store_hourly
WHERE store_id IN {stores:Array(UInt32)} AND hour >= {since:DateTime64(6)} AND big_orders > 10;

-- name: ItemTotals :many
SELECT item, sum(n) AS total
FROM item_daily
WHERE item IN ('pen', 'ink', 'pad') AND day >= {since:Date}
GROUP BY item;

-- name: CustomerTotal :one
SELECT spent, orders FROM customer_totals WHERE customer_id = {customer:String};

-- name: CustomerOrders :many
SELECT event_time, amount
FROM orders
WHERE customer_id = {customer:String}
ORDER BY event_time
LIMIT {n:UInt32};
-- explain orders --
stream orders
  assumptions  --rate 1000  --event-kb 1  --rows store_hourly=1000  --pass store_hourly=1  --rows item_daily=1000  --pass item_daily=1  --horizon 365d
               (traffic isn't in the SQL or dynaflux.yaml; set it with these flags)
  prices       us-east-1 list prices, ±30%: for comparing designs, not a quote; prices in eu-west-1 are higher

layout, in each of 2 regions (us-east-1, eu-west-1)
  table        shop-stream-orders: Global Table, on-demand
  key          pk customer_id and a shard of 4; sk event_time, event_id
  log          LOG RETENTION FOREVER, evaluated at --horizon 365d: ~34,690 GB in ~3,469 partitions, each a change-stream shard
  projector    batching window 1 s, batches of at most 100 events: ~869 invocations/s, 1.15 events each on average
  marks        a view row keeps a shard's high-water mark for 960 s after the shard's progress passes it (runtime.prune_window_seconds)
  archive      Firehose to Iceberg; each region archives the events it accepted

per event, per region                                                        write units  read units
  event put, local or replicated; a transaction where accepted (SYNC views)         1.50
  customer_totals, a row per entity, SYNC                                           1.65        1.10
  store_hourly, shared rows, 1,000 active, SPLIT 4                                 11.00        3.30
  item_daily, shared rows, 1,000 active                                            24.20        6.60
  progress item, one per batch                                                      0.87
  total                                                                            39.22       11.00
  per second, all events                                                          39,216      10,999

monthly                        per region  2 regions
  DynamoDB requests               $66,669   $133,338
    stream and meta tables         $3,891     $7,781
    customer_totals                $1,715     $3,430
    store_hourly                  $19,150    $38,300
    item_daily                    $41,913    $83,827
  DynamoDB storage of the log      $8,672    $17,345
  Lambda, the projector            $1,440     $2,880
  Firehose                            $71       $142
  S3 archive, year-1 average          $36        $73
  replication transfer                $26        $53
  total                           $76,915   $153,830
  provisioned capacity at a 70% target would cut requests to $28,521 per region (-57%), but costs more than on-demand if it must cover peaks over 2.42 times the average
  not modeled: query reads, view and marker storage, backfills, archive compaction, CloudWatch

warning orders: LOG RETENTION FOREVER keeps every event in DynamoDB: at this rate the log gains ~9.5 partitions a day, so batches shrink and projection costs more every day; this report is for --horizon 365d
  hint: keep a short log (the default is 2 DAY), and use the archive as the permanent record
warning orders: batches average 1.15 events over 3,469 shards, so view writes rarely combine: most events cost a row write in every view
  hint: shorten LOG RETENTION, which means fewer shards, or raise runtime.batching_window_seconds
warning orders: ~3,469 shards per region: after an outage, the projector catches up with one invocation per shard, over Lambda's default quota of 1,000 concurrent executions, which all of the account's functions in a region share
  hint: shorten LOG RETENTION, or raise the account's Lambda concurrency quota
warning store_hourly: rows reach 9.2 KB with ~224 high-water marks, one for each shard that wrote the row within the prune window (the stream has 3,469 shards); every write costs 10 write units
  hint: shorten LOG RETENTION on orders (fewer shards), or runtime.prune_window_seconds
warning item_daily: rows reach 21.1 KB with ~894 high-water marks, one for each shard that wrote the row within the prune window (the stream has 3,469 shards); every write costs 22 write units
  hint: shorten LOG RETENTION on orders (fewer shards), or runtime.prune_window_seconds
-- explain customer_totals --
view customer_totals, from stream orders
  assumptions  --rate 1000  --event-kb 1  --horizon 365d
               (traffic isn't in the SQL or dynaflux.yaml; set it with these flags)
  prices       us-east-1 list prices, ±30%: for comparing designs, not a quote; prices in eu-west-1 are higher

layout, in each of 2 regions (us-east-1, eu-west-1)
  table        shop-view-customer_totals-v1: regional, provisioned, autoscaling to 70% of reads and 50% of writes
  mode         SYNC: in the region that accepts an append, its rows change in the append's transaction, at twice the write units; other regions project it in batches
  rows         a row per entity: GROUP BY (customer_id) holds customer_id, the PRIMARY KEY of orders
  backfill     fenced handoff: sum, avg and count are additive

row, estimated (String values are taken as 32 bytes)
  pk           customer_id                                           34 B
  sk           a constant                                             3 B
  customer_id  key column                                            43 B
  spent        sum(amount)                                           17 B
  avg_price    avg(price)                                            29 B
  orders       count()                                               14 B
  _v           row version                                           10 B
  _hwm         ~4 high-water marks of ~24 B                         103 B
  total        1 write unit, 1 read unit; the item limit is 400 KB  253 B

per event, per region
  rows         1.65 write units and 1.10 read units: a batch of 1.15 events updates 0.576 rows, as the projector skips the appends that this region accepted, and 10% of writes conflict and retry
  monthly      DynamoDB requests $1,715 per region, $3,430 for 2 regions
-- explain store_hourly --
view store_hourly, from stream orders
  assumptions  --rate 1000  --event-kb 1  --rows store_hourly=1000  --pass store_hourly=1  --horizon 365d
               (traffic isn't in the SQL or dynaflux.yaml; set it with these flags)
  prices       us-east-1 list prices, ±30%: for comparing designs, not a quote; prices in eu-west-1 are higher

layout, in each of 2 regions (us-east-1, eu-west-1)
  table        shop-view-store_hourly-v1: regional, on-demand
  mode         ASYNC: the projector applies events in batches
  rows         shared: GROUP BY (store_id, hour) leaves out customer_id, the PRIMARY KEY of orders, so any shard can write any row; ~1,000 active
  split        SPLIT 4: each row is 4 items, chosen by shard; a query reads all 4
  backfill     fenced handoff: quantile and count are additive

row, estimated (String values are taken as 32 bytes)
  pk          store_id                                                                      6 B
  sk          hour and the item number                                                      7 B
  store_id    key column                                                                   14 B
  hour        key column                                                                   10 B
  customers   uniq(customer_id)                                                  2.5 KB at most
  p99         quantile(latency_ms)                                    ~1.37 KB, 39.6 KB at most
  big_orders  countIf(…)                                                                   18 B
  _v          row version                                                                  10 B
  _ttl        expiry                                                                       10 B
  _hwm        ~224 high-water marks of ~24 B                                            5.25 KB
  total       10 write units, 3 read units; the item limit is 400 KB                     9.2 KB

per event, per region
  rows         11.00 write units and 3.30 read units: a batch of 1.15 events updates 1.15 rows, and 10% of writes conflict and retry
  per row      ~2.75 write and 0.825 read units per second on average; a partition takes 1,000 and 3,000
  monthly      DynamoDB requests $19,150 per region, $38,300 for 2 regions

note p99: the row size takes a sketch of values that span a million-fold range; a wider spread makes it larger.

warning store_hourly: rows reach 9.2 KB with ~224 high-water marks, one for each shard that wrote the row within the prune window (the stream has 3,469 shards); every write costs 10 write units
  hint: shorten LOG RETENTION on orders (fewer shards), or runtime.prune_window_seconds
-- explain StoreHours --
query StoreHours :many, from view store_hourly
  assumptions  --rate 1000  --event-kb 1  --rows store_hourly=1000  --pass store_hourly=1  --horizon 365d
               (traffic isn't in the SQL or dynaflux.yaml; set it with these flags)
  prices       us-east-1 list prices, ±30%: for comparing designs, not a quote; prices in eu-west-1 are higher

  read         Query of store_id IN {stores} AND hour >= {since}
  keys         up to 100 key combinations: the Array parameters set them at call time
  calls        up to 100 Queries per call, one per key combination
  items        every row in the range, 4 items per row (SPLIT 4)
  read cost    9.2 KB per item, and a Query rounds its total up to 4 KB: ~1.15 read units per item eventually consistent, ~2.3 with WaitFor, which reads with strong consistency
  filter       the WHERE terms that bind no key run on the items read: they don't reduce the read or its cost
-- explain ItemTotals --
query ItemTotals :many, from view item_daily
  assumptions  --rate 1000  --event-kb 1  --rows item_daily=1000  --pass item_daily=1  --horizon 365d
               (traffic isn't in the SQL or dynaflux.yaml; set it with these flags)
  prices       us-east-1 list prices, ±30%: for comparing designs, not a quote; prices in eu-west-1 are higher

  read         Query of item IN ('pen', 'ink', 'pad') AND day >= {since}
  calls        3 Queries per call, one per key combination
  items        every row in the range
  read cost    21.1 KB per row, and a Query rounds its total up to 4 KB: ~2.63 read units per row eventually consistent, ~5.27 with WaitFor, which reads with strong consistency
  combine      GROUP BY item: sum(n)
-- explain CustomerTotal --
query CustomerTotal :one, from view customer_totals
  assumptions  --rate 1000  --event-kb 1  --horizon 365d
               (traffic isn't in the SQL or dynaflux.yaml; set it with these flags)
  prices       us-east-1 list prices, ±30%: for comparing designs, not a quote; prices in eu-west-1 are higher

  read         GetItem of customer_id = {customer}
  items        1 item per call
  read cost    0.247 KB per item, each rounded up to 4 KB: ~1 read unit per item, strongly consistent as the view is SYNC
-- explain CustomerOrders --
query CustomerOrders :many, from stream orders
  assumptions  --event-kb 1
               (traffic isn't in the SQL or dynaflux.yaml; set it with these flags)
  prices       us-east-1 list prices, ±30%: for comparing designs, not a quote; prices in eu-west-1 are higher

  read         Query of customer_id = {customer}
  calls        4 Queries per call, one per key combination and shard (SHARDS 4)
  items        every event of a key combination
  read cost    1 KB per event, and a Query rounds its total up to 4 KB: ~0.125 read units per event eventually consistent, ~0.25 strongly consistent
  limit        LIMIT {n} stops each Query after that many rows
